  "query_preview": "..While this evening's satellite imagery reveals\nlargely clear skies across the region, surface air quality sensors\naround Utah and southwest Wyoming continue to show evidence of\nwidespread smoke blanketed over the forecast area. That said,\nimprovements to smoke are expected over the next 24 to 48 hours as\na ridge axis begins to shift overhead, favoring deeper atmospheric\nmixing during the afternoon hours. \n\nAs this ridge begins to build into the region over the next couple\nof days, anticipate a stout daily warming trend (3-5 degrees each\nday) across the northern area, with a slower warming trend across\nthe southern area (1-2 degrees each day) through at least Friday.\nBy the end of the work week, high temperatures along the Wasatch\nFront will climb back into the triple digits, with Utah's Mojave\nreaching upwards of 110 to 112 degrees. Pairing with the hot\ndaytime temperatures, overnight lows will once again climb into\nthe low- to mid-70s across the western valley areas and around 80\ndegrees in the lower elevation valleys of southern Utah. While the\ndaytime high temperatures will be around 5-10 degrees above\naverage for this time of year, the lack of overnight relief is\nwhere the real concern will arise. A litany of heat advisories and\nextreme heat warnings will go into effect starting on Thursday in\nsouthern Utah, expanding into northern Utah by Friday as heat risk\nincreases due to the prolonged exposure to hot daytime\ntemperatures and elevated overnight lows. While Friday will mark\nthe peak in the anticipated heat, hot temperatures are expected to\nlinger through the weekend.\n\nIn addition to the heat, mid-level moisture is also expected to\nincrease across a portion of southern Utah tomorrow, leading to\nthe development of isolated high-based showers from roughly Bald\nMountain westward through the UT/NV border. Given the lack of\nlow-level moisture, any developing showers will bring potential\nfor strong microburst wind gusts in excess of 40-45 mph. As is the\ncase with a majority of the shower and thunderstorm activity in\nthe Great Basin region, anticipate development of these showers\nafter about noon, with the threat remaining in place through at\nleast the late evening. This high-based moisture will remain in\nplace through the weekend, bringing a daily threat for these\nmicroburst winds.\n\nAs the center of the high begins to shift farther east into early\nnext week, lower level moisture will begin increasing which will\nbring the increasing risk of heavy rainfall and flash flooding\nacross a portion of Utah, central and southern Utah in particular.\nWill continue to monitor this potential as we head through the\nweekend. At the bottom line, anticipate a return of monsoonal \nmoisture next week.",
